    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Long Road Almanac",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Long Road Almanac\"**\n\nThe site reads like a club almanac kept by people who ride all year: a calendar organized by month, routes catalogued like field notes, the landscape present in color and photographs. The palette comes from the region (lake water, hemlock, red barns, wheat and bark on warm paper) and is set on flat, quiet surfaces with no gloss. A bookish serif carries headings and the numbers riders scan for (the date, the distance). A plain sans carries everything else.\n\nDensity is moderate. The calendar and route table are compact enough to scan a season at a glance. Prose pages (About, Brevets, Lighting, Waterfalls) sit at a comfortable reading measure. Warmth comes from the materials (paper tones, serif numerals, real ride photos, the logo's bike against a sage panel), not from decoration or motion. The site is static and calm: almost no animation, only hover color shifts.\n\nLight and dark modes are both first-class. Dark mode follows the system setting and swaps every token for a night-paper equivalent; the hues stay the same and only lightness changes.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper ground, card-stock surfaces, bark-brown ink; never pure white or black.",
      "Fraunces for headings and for the numbers riders scan for; Inter for everything else.",
      "One cool primary (Seneca Lake) for action and links; Barn Rust for distances and status; Hemlock in small doses.",
      "Flat: depth comes from paper-versus-card tone and hairline borders, never shadows.",
      "Soft, plain shapes: pills for navigation and status, gently rounded cards and controls."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lake Rule",
        "body": "Seneca Lake is the only interactive color. If something is clickable it is Seneca Lake (or sits on Seneca Lake Wash); nothing else is.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Rust Means Distance Rule",
        "body": "Barn Rust marks the distance, the status, or the exception (draft, moved, cancelled, required). It is never used for links or buttons.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Pure Values Rule",
        "body": "No `#fff` or `#000` anywhere. Paper, card stock and bark ink are the extremes.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Serif Numbers Rule",
        "body": "The figures a rider scans for (date, distance, time limit) are set in Fraunces bold. Supporting numbers in tables and metadata stay in Inter.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Families Rule",
        "body": "Fraunces and Inter only. No third face, no monospace.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper and Card Rule",
        "body": "To lift something, put it on Card Stock with a Fence Line border. To highlight it, use Seneca Lake Wash. Never use a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use the CSS custom properties in `styles.css` (`--lake`, `--forest`, `--vine`, `--bg`, `--surface`, `--ink`, `--muted`, `--line`) rather than literal colors, so dark mode follows.",
      "**Do** set the date and distance riders scan for in Fraunces bold, with distance in Barn Rust.",
      "**Do** lift surfaces with Card Stock and a 1px Fence Line border, and highlight with Seneca Lake Wash.",
      "**Do** use pills for navigation, filters, tags and status, 8px for controls and 10px for containers.",
      "**Do** use real club ride photos from `site/assets/photos/`, cropped to a fixed aspect ratio.",
      "**Do** check every new element in both light and dark modes."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add box shadows, gradients or glossy fills.",
      "**Don't** use Barn Rust or Hemlock for links or general buttons; Seneca Lake is the interactive color.",
      "**Don't** use pure white or pure black.",
      "**Don't** introduce a third typeface.",
      "**Don't** recolor the logo outside its own variables, or use stock imagery in place of club photos."
    ]
  }
}